1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,866
In just three months this year,
one company sold one hundred

2
00:00:03,876 --> 00:00:06,723
and fifteen billion dollars
worth of computer chips.

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:08,491
That is almost double
what it earned

4
00:00:08,503 --> 00:00:11,459
in an entire year, just
three years earlier.

5
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:13,358
Its name is NVIDIA.

6
00:00:13,378 --> 00:00:16,060
And almost every major
artificial intelligence

7
00:00:16,072 --> 00:00:18,785
you have ever used was
built on its hardware.

8
00:00:19,013 --> 00:00:21,539
But this is not just
a story about fast chips.

9
00:00:21,559 --> 00:00:25,127
It is the story of how one
company rewired an entire

10
00:00:25,139 --> 00:00:28,763
industry, and why its grip
may finally be slipping.

11
00:00:29,047 --> 00:00:31,720
Here is how NVIDIA changed AI.

12
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:35,705
The story begins
in nineteen ninety-three,

13
00:00:35,725 --> 00:00:38,767
in a booth at a Denny's
diner near San Jose.

14
00:00:39,054 --> 00:00:41,194
Three engineers, Jensen Huang,

15
00:00:41,214 --> 00:00:43,561
Chris Malachowsky
and Curtis Priem,

16
00:00:43,581 --> 00:00:45,658
had a simple idea: build chips

17
00:00:45,668 --> 00:00:47,447
that make video games look better.

18
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:49,141
In nineteen ninety-nine,

19
00:00:49,161 --> 00:00:51,762
they released the GeForce
two fifty-six,

20
00:00:51,782 --> 00:00:55,646
and marketed it as the world's
first graphics processing unit.

21
00:00:55,666 --> 00:00:56,644
The GPU.

22
00:00:56,922 --> 00:00:58,977
For years, that was
the whole business.

23
00:00:58,997 --> 00:01:00,882
Gamers bought graphics cards,

24
00:01:00,902 --> 00:01:03,106
and NVIDIA kept
making them faster.

25
00:01:03,313 --> 00:01:05,490
Then, in two thousand
six, the company

26
00:01:05,502 --> 00:01:08,176
made a strange and expensive bet.

27
00:01:08,196 --> 00:01:10,970
It released a software
platform called CUDA.

28
00:01:11,247 --> 00:01:13,506
CUDA let scientists
use a graphics card

29
00:01:13,516 --> 00:01:15,683
for something other than graphics.

30
00:01:15,693 --> 00:01:19,195
Any heavy calculation
could now run on a GPU.

31
00:01:19,713 --> 00:01:21,260
Investors were skeptical.

32
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,869
It was expensive, and for years,

33
00:01:23,889 --> 00:01:25,693
few customers were asking for it.

34
00:01:25,947 --> 00:01:29,442
But in twenty twelve, three
researchers in Toronto used two

35
00:01:29,454 --> 00:01:33,491
NVIDIA gaming cards to train
a neural network called AlexNet.

36
00:01:33,747 --> 00:01:37,136
It crushed every rival
at recognizing images.

37
00:01:37,156 --> 00:01:39,238
And the modern AI boom began.

38
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:43,185
Four years later, Jensen Huang
personally delivered NVIDIA's

39
00:01:43,205 --> 00:01:48,176
first AI supercomputer to a small
research lab called OpenAI.

40
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:51,985
In twenty twenty-two,
that lab released ChatGPT.

41
00:01:52,005 --> 00:01:54,743
And demand for NVIDIA's
chips went vertical.

42
00:01:55,583 --> 00:01:57,773
So why did a chip built
for video games turn

43
00:01:57,783 --> 00:02:00,436
out to be perfect
for artificial intelligence?

44
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:05,632
A traditional processor, a CPU,
has a handful of powerful cores.

45
00:02:05,652 --> 00:02:08,111
It works through a job
one step at a time.

46
00:02:08,378 --> 00:02:10,212
Think of it as
a brilliant chef who

47
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:12,863
insists on cooking
every dish alone.

48
00:02:13,113 --> 00:02:14,986
A GPU is different.

49
00:02:15,006 --> 00:02:16,623
It has thousands of smaller cores

50
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:18,582
that all work at the same moment.

51
00:02:18,847 --> 00:02:20,938
It is a kitchen
with a thousand cooks,

52
00:02:20,958 --> 00:02:23,793
each doing one small
task, all at once.

53
00:02:24,047 --> 00:02:26,718
Give both the same
job, and the GPU

54
00:02:26,738 --> 00:02:29,233
is finished while the CPU
has barely started.

55
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,475
And a neural network
is exactly that kind of job:

56
00:02:32,495 --> 00:02:36,046
millions of simple calculations
that can all happen in parallel.

57
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:39,194
Training a model means
showing it data,

58
00:02:39,214 --> 00:02:40,893
measuring how wrong it is,

59
00:02:40,913 --> 00:02:44,051
and nudging billions of numbers
to be slightly less wrong.

60
00:02:44,313 --> 00:02:46,692
Repeat that trillions
of times, and you get

61
00:02:46,702 --> 00:02:49,088
a model that can write,
draw and reason.

62
00:02:49,347 --> 00:02:52,122
Using the finished model
is called inference.

63
00:02:52,142 --> 00:02:54,792
Every question you
type triggers billions

64
00:02:54,812 --> 00:02:57,853
more calculations,
on the same kind of chips.

65
00:02:58,113 --> 00:03:00,626
On ordinary processors,
training a frontier

66
00:03:00,638 --> 00:03:02,973
model would be impractically slow.

67
00:03:02,993 --> 00:03:06,702
On thousands of GPUs working
together, it takes weeks.

68
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:11,082
But speed alone does not
explain NVIDIA's dominance.

69
00:03:11,102 --> 00:03:13,051
Rivals can build fast chips too.

70
00:03:13,313 --> 00:03:16,918
The real advantage is everything
wrapped around the chip.

71
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:19,319
At the bottom is the hardware.

72
00:03:19,339 --> 00:03:22,401
Above it, complete server
systems and networking.

73
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:23,703
And above that, CUDA.

74
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:26,756
After twenty years,
more than eleven million

75
00:03:26,768 --> 00:03:29,749
developers build on it,
according to the company.

76
00:03:30,013 --> 00:03:31,716
Universities teach it.

77
00:03:31,736 --> 00:03:33,634
Research papers are
written with it.

78
00:03:33,654 --> 00:03:36,088
Entire startups are
built on top of it.

79
00:03:36,313 --> 00:03:40,321
NVIDIA also writes the libraries
on top: tools for chatbots,

80
00:03:40,341 --> 00:03:43,625
robots, self-driving
cars and drug discovery.

81
00:03:43,884 --> 00:03:45,877
So switching away
from NVIDIA does not

82
00:03:45,887 --> 00:03:47,711
just mean buying a different chip.

83
00:03:47,731 --> 00:03:50,020
It means rewriting
years of software.

84
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:52,882
That is why analysts estimate
NVIDIA still collects

85
00:03:52,892 --> 00:03:56,553
around three quarters of all
spending on AI chips.

86
00:03:57,383 --> 00:04:00,393
And NVIDIA no longer
sells just chips.

87
00:04:00,413 --> 00:04:03,078
It sells entire AI factories.

88
00:04:03,347 --> 00:04:06,435
Its newest system,
called Vera Rubin,

89
00:04:06,455 --> 00:04:08,704
is not a card you
slot into a computer.

90
00:04:08,724 --> 00:04:11,993
It is a complete rack
of seventy-two GPUs,

91
00:04:12,013 --> 00:04:13,804
cooled entirely by liquid.

92
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:17,947
One rack delivers roughly
three times the AI

93
00:04:17,967 --> 00:04:20,607
computing power of the
generation before it.

94
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:23,903
And a single rack can draw
around two hundred kilowatts

95
00:04:23,913 --> 00:04:27,258
of electricity, as much as
well over a hundred homes.

96
00:04:27,494 --> 00:04:30,034
Fill a building
with thousands of these racks

97
00:04:30,054 --> 00:04:33,008
and you get what the company
calls an AI factory.

98
00:04:33,028 --> 00:04:35,289
Electricity and data go in.

99
00:04:35,309 --> 00:04:36,712
Intelligence comes out.

100
00:04:36,947 --> 00:04:39,098
Every answer an AI gives is made

101
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:41,724
of tokens, small pieces of words.

102
00:04:41,744 --> 00:04:44,737
An AI factory produces
them by the trillion.

103
00:04:45,013 --> 00:04:48,260
The chips are designed in California,
but most are manufactured

104
00:04:48,270 --> 00:04:52,343
in Taiwan, using machines
and materials from all over the world.

105
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:55,863
Every one begins as a wafer
of silicon, printed

106
00:04:55,873 --> 00:04:59,181
with features just a few
billionths of a meter wide.

107
00:04:59,447 --> 00:05:02,892
And NVIDIA now launches
a new generation roughly

108
00:05:02,904 --> 00:05:06,248
every year, faster than
most rivals can respond.

109
00:05:07,083 --> 00:05:08,920
The result is one of the fastest

110
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:11,328
growth stories
in business history.

111
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,952
In fiscal twenty
twenty-three, NVIDIA's

112
00:05:13,962 --> 00:05:16,612
revenue was twenty-seven
billion dollars.

113
00:05:16,632 --> 00:05:18,824
A year later, sixty-one billion.

114
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:20,692
Then one hundred
and thirty billion.

115
00:05:20,712 --> 00:05:22,685
Then two hundred
and sixteen billion.

116
00:05:22,947 --> 00:05:24,709
And in its latest quarter alone,

117
00:05:24,729 --> 00:05:27,027
revenue reached one
hundred and fifteen point

118
00:05:27,047 --> 00:05:29,219
eight billion dollars,
with ninety-three

119
00:05:29,231 --> 00:05:31,289
percent coming from data centers.

120
00:05:31,547 --> 00:05:34,855
And the company expects to sell
even more next quarter:

121
00:05:34,875 --> 00:05:37,237
about one hundred
and thirty billion dollars.

122
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:41,099
Its gross margin is close
to seventy-five percent.

123
00:05:41,119 --> 00:05:44,412
For every dollar of sales,
about seventy-five cents

124
00:05:44,432 --> 00:05:47,158
is left after the cost
of making the product.

125
00:05:47,413 --> 00:05:49,043
Who is paying for all this?

126
00:05:49,063 --> 00:05:51,267
Mostly, a handful of giants.

127
00:05:51,513 --> 00:05:55,028
Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft
and Meta are on track

128
00:05:55,038 --> 00:05:56,753
to spend around seven
hundred and thirty

129
00:05:56,773 --> 00:05:59,132
billion dollars
on infrastructure this year,

130
00:05:59,152 --> 00:06:01,489
and a large share
of it flows to NVIDIA.

131
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:04,446
The biggest technology
companies on Earth are building

132
00:06:04,459 --> 00:06:08,544
as fast as they can, and NVIDIA
is their main supplier.

133
00:06:08,813 --> 00:06:11,818
That river of money pushed
NVIDIA's market value

134
00:06:11,828 --> 00:06:15,303
above five trillion dollars
in twenty twenty-six.

135
00:06:15,323 --> 00:06:18,231
In October, it came
close to six trillion.

136
00:06:19,083 --> 00:06:21,388
But no empire is safe forever.

137
00:06:21,408 --> 00:06:24,729
And NVIDIA now faces
three serious threats.

138
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,031
First, its own customers.

139
00:06:27,051 --> 00:06:29,794
Google, Amazon and others
are designing custom

140
00:06:29,806 --> 00:06:32,689
AI chips to cut their
dependence on NVIDIA.

141
00:06:32,947 --> 00:06:36,829
AMD is chasing too,
with its own accelerators

142
00:06:36,849 --> 00:06:38,701
and a growing list of customers.

143
00:06:38,947 --> 00:06:40,834
And shipments of those
custom chips are

144
00:06:40,844 --> 00:06:44,953
growing nearly three
times as fast as GPUs.

145
00:06:45,213 --> 00:06:47,007
Second, geopolitics.

146
00:06:47,027 --> 00:06:50,478
Export restrictions have all
but closed the Chinese market,

147
00:06:50,498 --> 00:06:52,699
and NVIDIA's latest
forecast assumes

148
00:06:52,719 --> 00:06:55,226
zero data center
chip sales to China.

149
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,517
And third, leadership.

150
00:06:57,537 --> 00:07:01,252
In November twenty twenty-six,
Jensen Huang, the founder who led

151
00:07:01,262 --> 00:07:03,730
the company for more
than three decades,

152
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:06,063
stepped down as chief executive.

153
00:07:06,313 --> 00:07:10,115
Chief financial officer Colette
Kress became interim CEO,

154
00:07:10,135 --> 00:07:12,615
and the shares dropped
sharply on the news.

155
00:07:12,635 --> 00:07:15,936
By late November, NVIDIA's
market value was near

156
00:07:15,948 --> 00:07:20,008
four trillion dollars,
well below its October peak.

157
00:07:20,254 --> 00:07:22,794
For the first time,
the company has to prove

158
00:07:22,814 --> 00:07:24,836
it is bigger than
the man who built it.

159
00:07:25,684 --> 00:07:29,114
So, how is NVIDIA
changing the AI industry?

160
00:07:29,380 --> 00:07:31,545
It did it by betting
on an idea years

161
00:07:31,565 --> 00:07:33,939
before almost anyone
else believed in it.

162
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:35,788
It turned the graphics card into

163
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:38,149
the engine of a new
kind of computing.

164
00:07:38,413 --> 00:07:40,635
It turned software
into a moat that

165
00:07:40,647 --> 00:07:43,203
rivals still struggle to cross.

166
00:07:43,447 --> 00:07:44,976
And it turned the data center into

167
00:07:44,986 --> 00:07:48,076
a factory that
manufactures intelligence.

168
00:07:48,313 --> 00:07:51,656
Whether NVIDIA stays on top
is now an open question.

169
00:07:51,676 --> 00:07:54,584
But the industry it
built is not going away.

170
00:07:54,847 --> 00:07:58,115
Every model you talk to,
every image you generate,

171
00:07:58,135 --> 00:08:02,815
every AI tool you use, runs
on foundations that NVIDIA laid.

172
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:07,374
If this helped you understand
the machine behind AI, subscribe,

173
00:08:07,394 --> 00:08:09,489
and we will take apart
the next piece of the puzzle.
